## Why This Is Asked
This problem combines two key challenges: optimizing over two sequences simultaneously and selecting exactly k non-overlapping intervals. It tests your ability to formulate dynamic programming solutions for constrained partitioning problems and handle solution reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- 2D prefix sums for O(1) range sum queries
- Non-overlapping interval DP with exact count constraint
- Path reconstruction via backpointers
- Trade-off between intervals via knapsack-like combining

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxIntervalValue(A, B, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const PA = [0], PB = [0];
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    PA[i] = PA[i-1] + A[i-1];
    PB[i] = PB[i-1] + B[i-1];
  }
  const dp = Array(n+1).fill(null).map(() => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prev = Array(n+1).fill(null).map(() => Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let r = 1; r <= n; r++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      for (let l = 1; l <= r; l++) {
        const val = (PA[r] - PA[l-1]) * (PB[r] - PB[l-1]);
        const candidate = dp[l-1][t-1] + val;
        if (candidate > dp[r][t]) {
          dp[r][t] = candidate;
          prev[r][t] = { l, r: l-1 };
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n][k];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to derive a multi-interval DP with per-interval cost and reconstruction. Requires careful state definition and an O(nk) optimization akin to advanced interval DP.

## Key Concepts

- DP over prefixes and interval counts
- Prefix sums for subarray sums
- Running maximum optimization to avoid O(n^2k)
- Path reconstruction to recover subarray boundaries

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const prv = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let t=1; t<=k; t++) {
    let best = NEG;
    for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
      const val = dp[i-1][t-1] - S[i-1] + c*(i-1);
      if (val > best) { best = val; }
      const cand = S[i] - c*i + best;
      if (cand > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = cand; prv[i][t] = i; }
      if (dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t]; prv[i][t] = -1; }
    }
  }
  // reconstruct (simple backtrack)
  const intervals = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
    if (prv[i][t] === -1) { i--; continue; }
    const end = i;
    const start = prv[i][t];
    intervals.push([start, end]);
    i = start - 1; t--;
  }
  intervals.reverse();
  return { value: dp[n][k], intervals };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to allow variable per-interval cost or rewards based on length?
- How would you optimize memory to O(nk) and still reconstruct intervals?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model multi-block dynamic programming with non-trivial block scoring that depends on range statistics (max, min) and block length. It also covers reconstruction of the exact blocks, and invites discussion of time/space trade-offs.

## Key Concepts

- Partition DP with fixed k blocks
- Block score uses range (max-min) and length
- Inline maintenance of max/min while expanding the last block
- Path reconstruction with predecessor pointers

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxScore(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      let curMax = -INF, curMin = INF;
      for (let l = i; l >= 1; l--) {
        curMax = Math.max(curMax, A[l-1]);
        curMin = Math.min(curMin, A[l-1]);
        const blockScore = (curMax - curMin) * (i - l + 1);
        if (dp[l-1][t-1] + blockScore > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = dp[l-1][t-1] + blockScore;
          parent[i][t] = l-1;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct blocks
  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const l = parent[i][t] + 1;
    blocks.unshift([l, i]);
    i = l - 1;
    t--;
  }

  return {maxScore: dp[n][k], blocks};
}
```

## Time/Space Complexity

Time: O(n²k) - for each i and t, we scan O(n) possible l values while maintaining max/min.

Space: O(nk) for dp table plus O(nk) for parent pointers if reconstruction needed, otherwise O(nk).

## Why This Is Asked

This tests a standard DP with an adjacency constraint and a limit on picks, plus reconstruction.

## Key Concepts

- DP over i and t with i-2 due to adjacency
- Reconstruction via a take/parent trace
- Space/time trade-offs; potential optimizations

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxNonAdjSum(a, k) {
  const n = a.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(0));
  const take = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=>Array(k+1).fill(false));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      if (t > 0 && i >= 2 && dp[i-2][t-1] + a[i-1] > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-2][t-1] + a[i-1];
        take[i][t] = true;
      }
    }
  }
  // Reconstruct indices
  const indices = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    if (take[i][t]) {
      indices.push(i-1);
      i -= 2;
      t--;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }
  return { maxProfit: dp[n][k], indices: indices.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests precise DP construction for selecting k disjoint subarrays with per-subarray penalty, a common optimization variant.

## Key Concepts
- Disjoint subarray DP with two states
- Transition derivation for starting/continuing a subarray
- Space/time trade-offs and reconstruction

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const end = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const best = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  best[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
    for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) {
      end[i][t] = Math.max(end[i-1][t] + A[i-1], t>0 ? best[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1] : NEG);
      best[i][t] = Math.max(best[i-1][t], end[i][t] - c);
    }
  }
  return {value: best[n][k], subs: null};
}
```

