## Why This Problem Is Asked
This problem tests your ability to design multi-dimensional DP by combining deletion constraints with pairwise scoring across adjacent characters. It evaluates state space design, transition logic, and reconstruction from a full solution path.

## Key Concepts
- **State design**: A 3D DP tracks position i (0 to n), deletions used d (0 to k), and the last kept character L (0–25, or 26 for none).
- **Transition logic**: The delete transition preserves state; the keep transition accumulates score only when a previous character exists, using W[prev][cur].
- **Reconstruction**: Backtracking from the optimal endpoint to determine which characters were deleted.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxScore(s, W, k) {
    const n = s.length, NONE = 26;
    const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () =>
        Array.from({length: k+1}, () =>
            Array(NONE+1).fill(-Infinity)));
    dp[0][0][NONE] = 0;

    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for (let d = 0; d <= k; d++) {
            for (let L = 0; L <= NONE; L++) {
                if (dp[i][d][L] === -Infinity) continue;
                // Delete
                if (d < k) dp[i+1][d+1][L] = Math.max(dp[i+1][d+1][L], dp[i][d][L]);
                // Keep
                const cur = s.charCodeAt(i) - 97;
                const gain = L === NONE ? 0 : W[L][cur];
                dp[i+1][d][cur] = Math.max(dp[i+1][d][cur], dp[i][d][L] + gain);
            }
        }
    }
    // Find best among d ≤ k
    let best = 0, bestD = 0, bestL = NONE;
    for (let d = 0; d <= k; d++)
        for (let L = 0; L <= NONE; L++)
            if (dp[n][d][L] > best) { best = dp[n][d][L]; bestD = d; bestL = L; }
    return { score: best, deletions: bestD, lastKept: bestL };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- **Memory optimization**: Can you reduce space to O(27·k) using rolling arrays?
- **Negative weights**: How would you handle cases where W contains negative values—would your approach change?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over partitioning with a quadratic segment cost, requiring prefix sums, base cases, and reconstruction, plus discussion of optimizations.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions: dp[i][t] for first i elements into t segments
- Prefix sums for O(1) segment sums
- Quadratic segment cost and optimization (CHT or Divide-and-Conquer)
- Reconstructing segment boundaries with a prev table
- Time/space trade-offs and edge cases

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const S = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) S[i] = S[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(INF));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n+1 }, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k && t <= i; t++) {
      for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
        const diff = S[i] - S[j];
        const cost = diff * diff;
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + cost;
        if (val < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          prev[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return { cost: dp[n][k] };
}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked
Tests the ability to extend classic path DP problems with an additional resource constraint, evaluating proficiency in managing 3D DP states, path reconstruction via backtracking, and space optimization.

## Key Concepts
- 3D DP state (i, j, t) tracking exact t boosts used at position (i,j)
- Two transition options: boost or skip the current cell
- Path reconstruction by backtracking through the DP table
- Space optimization: reduce to two rows or maintain a compact parent pointer table

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithBoosts(grid, k, B) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: m }, () => new Array(k + 1).fill(NEG))
  );
  dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  if (k > 0) dp[0][0][1] = grid[0][0] + B;

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        if (i > 0 && dp[i-1][j][t] !== NEG) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i-1][j][t] + grid[i][j]);
        }
        if (j > 0 && dp[i][j-1][t] !== NEG) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i][j-1][t] + grid[i][j]);
        }
        if (t > 0) {
          if (i > 0 && dp[i-1][j][t-1] !== NEG) {
            dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i-1][j][t-1] + grid[i][j] + B);
          }
          if (j > 0 && dp[i][j-1][t-1] !== NEG) {
            dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i][j-1][t-1] + grid[i][j] + B);
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const boosts = [];
  let i = n - 1, j = m - 1, t = k;
  while (i > 0 || j > 0) {
    const fromTop = i > 0 && dp[i][j][t] === dp[i-1][j][t] + grid[i][j];
    const fromLeft = j > 0 && dp[i][j][t] === dp[i][j-1][t] + grid[i][j];
    const boostedFromTop = i > 0 && t > 0 && dp[i][j][t] === dp[i-1][j][t-1] + grid[i][j] + B;
    const boostedFromLeft = j > 0 && t > 0 && dp[i][j][t] === dp[i][j-1][t-1] + grid[i][j] + B;

    if (boostedFromTop || boostedFromLeft) boosts.push([i, j]);
    if (boostedFromTop || (i > 0 && fromTop && !boostedFromTop)) { i--; }
    else if (j > 0) { j--; if (!boostedFromLeft && !fromLeft) t--; }
  }

  return { maxSum: dp[n-1][m-1][k], boosts: boosts.reverse() };
}
```

## Complexity
- **Time:** O(n × m × k)
- **Space:** O(n × m × k) or O(m × k) with row optimization

## Why This Is Asked
DP over subsequences with a fixed length captures real-world problems like quantizing sequences while minimizing total variation; it tests state definition, reconstruction, and complexity trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- DP state: last index and length; - Transition: O(n) over prior indices; - Reconstruction via parent pointers; - Complexity: O(n^2 k) time, O(nk) space.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostSubsequence(A, k) {
  const n = A.length, INF = 1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(INF));
  const par = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[i][1] = 0;
  for (let t=2;t<=k;t++) {
    for (let i=0;i<n;i++) {
      for (let j=0;j<i;j++) {
        const val = dp[j][t-1] + Math.abs(A[i]-A[j]);
        if (val < dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = j; }
      }
    }
  }
  let best = INF, last = -1;
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) if (dp[i][k] < best) { best = dp[i][k]; last = i; }
  const idx = Array(k);
  let t = k, cur = last;
  while (t>=1) { idx[t-1] = cur; cur = par[cur][t]; t--; }
  return {cost: best, indices: idx};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP formulation for selecting fixed-count non-overlapping subarrays with a per-block cost, plus reconstruction of blocks.

## Key Concepts

- DP over i (prefix) and t (blocks)
- Prefix sums for subarray sums
- Running best to avoid O(n^2)
- Reconstructing start/end indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarraysWithCost(A, k, c){
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const from = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  const bestVal = new Array(k+1).fill(NEG);
  const bestIdx = new Array(k+1).fill(-1);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++){
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      from[i][t] = {type:'skip', at:i-1, t};
    }
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++){
      const val = dp[i-1][t-1] - pref[i-1];
      if (val > bestVal[t]) { bestVal[t] = val; bestIdx[t] = i-1; }
      const cand = bestVal[t] + pref[i] - c;
      if (cand > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = cand;
        from[i][t] = {type:'end', end:i-1, start:bestIdx[t], t};
      }
    }
  }
  let i = n, t = k;
  const blocks = [];
  while (i > 0 && t > 0){
    const info = from[i][t];
    if (!info) break;
    if (info.type === 'end'){
      blocks.push([info.start, info.end]);
      i = info.start;
      t--;
    } else {
      i = info.at;
    }
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for at-most-k blocks?
- How would you handle negative values of c or allow zero-cost blocks?
- What modifications optimize space to O(k) while preserving reconstruction?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a three-dimensional DP that models constrained path planning with a binary obstacle grid and a budget of unblocks. Requires careful state definition, transitions, and path reconstruction. 

## Key Concepts
- DP with an extra dimension for unblocks (t)
- Handling blocked vs unblocked cells in transitions
- Path reconstruction from 3D DP table
- Time/space trade-offs and rolling optimization

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPath(grid, blocked, k) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: m}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG)));
  if (!blocked[0][0]) dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  else if (k > 0) dp[0][0][1] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        const val = grid[i][j];
        if (i > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i-1][j][t] + val * (blocked[i][j] ? 0 : 1));
          if (blocked[i][j] && t > 0) dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i-1][j][t-1] + val);
        }
        if (j > 0) {
          dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i][j-1][t] + val * (blocked[i][j] ? 0 : 1));
          if (blocked[i][j] && t > 0) dp[i][j][t] = Math.max(dp[i][j][t], dp[i][j-1][t-1] + val);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[n-1][m-1][0];
}
